  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe gradual cooling trend continues into the end of the week, \nwith persistent low (less than 25%) rain chances. However, highs \nstill warm into the 85F-95F range, 100F-105F along the Rio Grande \nThursday and Friday, as a relative lull in higher shower/storm \nchances continues. This will be largely due to continued mid-level\ncapping and drier air mixing down the surface, despite prolonged \nhumid, upslope southeast flow. As a result, 15 to 25 mph winds \neach afternoon through late evening can continue to be expected, \nwhile lows stay in the 60s and 70s F. It is not out of the \nquestion that an isolated storm or two may produce brief heavy \nrainfall, strong winds, and frequent lightning, but the chance \nwill be less than earlier this week. \n\nThis changes by next week. Spread in temperatures is still low \nthrough Saturday, with most recent forecast runs having highs \nstill into the 85F-95F range on Sunday. However, beginning \nSaturday, forecast runs still show PoPs increasing to the 35% to \n45% range over the western higher terrain into SE NM plains and \nPermian Basin ahead of a cold front developing in from the north. \nAccompanying the frontal passage by Sunday, higher PoPs expand \neast/southeast over the rest of the forecast area. Persistent \nsouth/southeast winds that have delivered summer-like temperatures\nfor the month so far are finally interrupted by a shift to more \neast/northeast winds behind the cold front. This will allow highs \nto drop into the 85F-95F range by early next week. Lows by early \nnext week are also expected to fall into the 50s and 60s F as \ncooler daytime temperatures below 90F allow nighttime temperatures\nto cool below 70F. At this time, there is no indication of \nwidespread severe weather accompanying the increased rain chances.\nHowever, due to ensembles showing both mean rain totals through \nearly next week up to 1.00\" over the western higher terrain and SE\nNM plains along with gradually increasing shear that can better \norganize storms, we will continue to monitor trends in both flash \nflooding and severe storm risk for this weekend through next week.\nThis will become even more important to watch as summer weather \nfades and risk of more widespread severe weather becomes \nincreasingly possible following transition into the more active \nautumn secondary severe weather season.",
